WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.283
A monolithic application is one principal
deployable application, not necessarily one

00:00:05.283 --> 00:00:10.814
physical machine. In the historical encyclopedia
design, many interchangeable workers run

00:00:10.814 --> 00:00:16.158
the same application code, each capable of
handling the application's request types. A

00:00:16.158 --> 00:00:21.876
caching proxy in front and a database behind do
not automatically turn that application into

00:00:21.876 --> 00:00:22.746
microservices.

00:00:22.746 --> 00:00:28.005
The diagram shows one application artifact
copied to several workers. Replication increases

00:00:28.005 --> 00:00:33.323
the capacity for independent requests while
keeping the code and release unit together. This

00:00:33.323 --> 00:00:38.466
distinction prevents a common category error:
counting deployment machines tells us about

00:00:38.466 --> 00:00:43.610
runtime topology, while identifying
independently developed and deployed
responsibilities

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:46.789
tells us about the structure of the application
itself.

00:00:46.789 --> 00:00:52.030
For a small team, keeping related functionality
in one application can make development

00:00:52.030 --> 00:00:57.031
practical. A developer runs one application
locally, steps through one request in a

00:00:57.031 --> 00:01:02.333
debugger, and can coordinate a database
transaction without crossing service boundaries.

00:01:02.333 --> 00:01:07.467
In the diagram, catalogue lookup, reservation
validation, and reservation storage are

00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:12.721
modules inside the same deployable boundary.
They should still have clear interfaces; a

00:01:12.721 --> 00:01:18.157
monolith need not be an undifferentiated pile of
code. The simplicity comes from sharing a

00:01:18.157 --> 00:01:21.962
runtime and deployment process, not from
abandoning modularity.

00:01:21.962 --> 00:01:26.724
Before splitting the system, identify a concrete
limitation that separate services would

00:01:26.724 --> 00:01:31.324
address and compare that benefit with the new
coordination work the split introduces.

00:01:31.462 --> 00:01:36.514
A service boundary turns a local collaboration
into an explicit network contract. Imagine

00:01:36.514 --> 00:01:41.510
separating a shop's catalogue from its order
workflow because they have distinct owners,

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:46.278
change patterns, and scaling needs. The order
service asks the catalogue for product

00:01:46.278 --> 00:01:51.103
information through an API rather than reading
its private tables or calling internal

00:01:51.103 --> 00:01:51.670
functions.

00:01:51.670 --> 00:01:56.498
The diagram marks both the request boundary and
the data owned behind it. Each side can

00:01:56.498 --> 00:02:01.437
change its implementation while preserving the
contract, but only if callers respect that

00:02:01.437 --> 00:02:06.487
boundary. If every release still requires
coordinated changes to both services, the
network

00:02:06.487 --> 00:02:11.593
split has not delivered much independence and
may simply have made an existing coupling more

00:02:11.593 --> 00:02:12.148
expensive.

00:02:12.170 --> 00:02:17.563
Splitting a checkout across services also
changes failures. A payment service might accept
a

00:02:17.563 --> 00:02:22.487
charge before an inventory reservation fails,
leaving a partially completed business

00:02:22.487 --> 00:02:27.235
operation. There is no automatic single-process
rollback across those independent

00:02:27.235 --> 00:02:27.879
boundaries.

00:02:27.879 --> 00:02:33.087
The diagram stops the inventory branch after
payment succeeds and then adds a compensating

00:02:33.087 --> 00:02:38.295
action such as releasing or refunding the
payment under a defined policy. This is a design

00:02:38.295 --> 00:02:42.634
decision, not a generic promise that
compensation perfectly erases history.

00:02:42.879 --> 00:02:48.549
Timeouts, duplicate delivery, and uncertain
outcomes need explicit handling. Follow the full

00:02:48.549 --> 00:02:53.972
operation rather than evaluating each small
service only in isolation, because customers

00:02:53.972 --> 00:02:55.882
experience the combined result.
